from schema import P, C, S

PART = P(
    "Recovery Ko Life Mein Convert Karo",
    "Thirty days ka start, ninety days ka rhythm, long-term choice",
    "08-relapse-reset.jpg",
    "Recovery ka goal har second urge ke baare mein sochna nahi. Goal hai aisi daily life jismein health, relationships, work, meaning and support itne real hon ki old pattern ko har decision ka centre na banna pade. Yeh part sustainable rhythm aur 30-day workbook ka safe use explain karta hai.",
    C(
        17,
        "Ninety-Day Rhythm Aur Long-Term Maintenance",
        "Identity ko intensity se nahi, repeated actions se banao",
        S(
            "Identity ko behaviour ke chhote proofs do",
            "‘Main ab completely new person hoon’ excitement de sakta hai, but fragile bhi. Better identity statements observable: ‘Main early help maangta hoon,’ ‘Main high-risk situation mein pause karta hoon,’ ‘Main prescribed treatment follow karta hoon,’ ‘Main consent aur agreements respect karta hoon.’ Har small action identity ko proof deta hai. Lapse proof ko erase nahi karta; it signals need for more support.",
            "Avoid recovery becoming your only identity. You are also a friend, learner, worker, creator, parent, partner, community member. Meaningful roles reduce the space available for compulsive pattern and make life rewarding. Do not overload yourself with big achievements to prove worth. Choose one role each week and one action that expresses it. Stable identity grows quietly.",
            exercise=("Identity evidence", ["I am a person who ___", "This week’s smallest proof: ___", "A non-recovery role I value: ___", "One action expressing that role: ___", "Support I will use if proof feels difficult: ___"]),
        ),
        S(
            "Four weekly anchors",
            "Anchor one: health/treatment — appointments, prescribed plan, sleep/meals/movement as appropriate. Anchor two: connection — one meaningful contact and one recurring group/activity. Anchor three: responsibility — work/study/home essentials. Anchor four: restoration — enjoyable, calming or spiritual practice aligned with your values, without becoming compulsory. These anchors create rhythm even when motivation fluctuates.",
            "Weekly schedule ko overcrowded mat banao. Put anchors at realistic times and leave transition space. Early recovery mein fewer commitments with support better than ambitious collapse. Review health needs with clinician before exercise or major routine changes. If a week goes off track, restore the anchor with highest safety impact first: treatment and immediate safety, then connection, responsibility, restoration.",
            bullets=["Health/treatment anchor: what and when?", "Connection anchor: who/where?", "Responsibility anchor: one essential commitment.", "Restoration anchor: one safe activity.", "Backup version for a difficult week."],
        ),
        S(
            "Rewards ko healthy aur flexible rakho",
            "Behaviour change ke liye healthy rewards useful hain: a meal you enjoy, time for hobby, a small purchase within budget, social outing, or acknowledging progress. Reward should not recreate the old risk, financial harm or another compulsion. Avoid using sexual access, substances, gambling or excessive spending as reward. Also avoid making basic needs conditional on perfect behaviour.",
            "Contingency management clinical settings mein structured reinforcement use karta hai, especially stimulant-use disorders mein. Home version simple and non-punitive ho sakta hai: appointment attend → planned enjoyable activity; week of support check-ins → shared outing. Do not withhold medicine, food, affection, or essential care for missed targets. Reward actions, not only abstinence, so effort remains visible even when outcome imperfect.",
            callout=("Good reward test", "Safe? Affordable? Values-aligned? Not another compulsive cycle? Not controlled as humiliation by someone else? Then it may be useful."),
        ),
        S(
            "Transitions ko high-risk maano, weakness nahi",
            "New job, exams, travel, festival, wedding, breakup, grief, illness, financial change, moving home, relationship shift, or medication/health change can disrupt routine. Even positive transitions increase decision load and access to new cues. Before a known transition, update the map: where will I stay, who is support, what access changes, what sleep/routine changes, what appointments continue?",
            "Travel ke liye prescribed medicines original packaging/documentation and local rules clinician/pharmacist se confirm karo; do not improvise. Digital compulsion mein hotel privacy, idle time, new device or unrestricted network risk badha sakta hai. Social events mein substance offers and consent risks plan karo. Increase support temporarily. Transition plan is not pessimism; it protects the progress you have built.",
            exercise=("Transition card", ["What changes in place, people, time and access?", "What remains stable?", "Which support increases temporarily?", "What is my exit/backup plan?", "Which early warning sign means professional contact?"]),
        ),
        S(
            "Early warning signs ko personal banao",
            "Generic list ke saath apne early signs identify karo: stopping appointments, secrecy, sleep change, irritability, romanticising old behaviour, reconnecting risky contacts, bypassing blockers, hiding spending, skipping meals, withdrawing from safe people, or ‘main ab cured hoon, support ki zaroorat nahi.’ One sign does not prove relapse; several together deserve review.",
            "Create three zones. Green: stable, routine working. Amber: early signs, increase contact and reduce access. Red: lapse, medical/mental-health risk, or danger to others — urgent professional/emergency response. Write actions for each zone in advance. Share appropriate version with support person so they can respond without guessing. Review zones after every significant event.",
            bullets=["GREEN: maintain routine and scheduled review.", "AMBER: contact support, simplify week, reinforce barriers, consider clinician review.", "RED: immediate safety, urgent professional/emergency help, no solo management.", "After RED: re-entry and plan revision, not punishment."],
        ),
        S(
            "Helping others without losing your own footing",
            "Recovery ke baad others ko help karna meaningful ho sakta hai, but too early over-responsibility stress create kar sakti hai. Share resources and your experience without claiming universal cure or acting as doctor/therapist. Do not collect others’ graphic histories, promise confidentiality you cannot maintain, or manage emergencies alone. Point to qualified services and protect your own routines.",
            "Peer support mein boundaries: time limits, no loans/procurement, no romantic/sexual exploitation, no medication sharing, no public disclosure. If another person is in danger, involve emergency/professional support. Your recovery does not require becoming a full-time rescuer. Sustainable helping is respectful, bounded and evidence-aware.",
            callout=("Useful help", "‘Main yeh verified resource share kar sakta hoon aur appointment tak support kar sakta hoon’ is safer than ‘Main tumhe cure kar dunga.’"),
        ),
        refs=["R10", "R11", "R12", "R14", "R17", "R18", "R23", "R24", "R26"],
    ),
    C(
        18,
        "30-Day Workbook Kaise Use Karein",
        "Structure do, perfection nahi",
        S(
            "Day zero: safety gate",
            "Workbook start karne se pehle decide karo ki self-guided exercises appropriate hain ya professional assessment first. Heavy alcohol/sedative use, previous severe withdrawal, opioid/mixed use, current severe symptoms, pregnancy/serious illness, self-harm/suicidal thoughts, violence/non-consensual risk, or inability to stay safe ho toh workbook ko treatment replacement mat banao. Contact appropriate service; exercises can support care after safety planning.",
            "Substance aur compulsion dono patterns hon toh sab kuch simultaneously perfect karne ka goal mat rakho. Highest safety risk primary track banega. Aap dono ke liye notes rakh sakte ho, but one main behaviour target at a time often more manageable. Clinician se priorities align karo. Day one perfect abstinence promise nahi; accurate baseline and safe next step hai.",
            callout=("Do not delay care", "Workbook completion ke liye doctor appointment postpone mat karo. Emergency signs mein page band karo aur help lo."),
        ),
        S(
            "Track S, Track M, ya combined support",
            "**Track S** substance-use recovery ke liye hai: medical/treatment plan, triggers, access, support, craving, lapse safety. **Track M** compulsive masturbation/porn pattern ke liye: values-based goal, impaired control, digital/emotional cues, consent/relationships, professional review. Dono tracks common skills share karte hain, but daily prompt mein apna relevant version choose karo.",
            "Combined pattern ho toh substance use ke time consent risk, sexual behaviour and overdose/withdrawal safety interconnected ho sakte hain. Professional assessment important. Workbook mein dono prompts complete karna compulsory nahi; primary risk pehle. Avoid using one behaviour to manage withdrawal or the other compulsion. Integrated treatment can address both without setting them against each other.",
            bullets=["Track S: follow medical/treatment instructions before self-help.", "Track M: no diagnosis from frequency or moral guilt alone.", "Combined: choose highest safety priority; disclose both to clinician.", "Every day: one action and one honest reflection is enough."],
        ),
        S(
            "Daily page ka rhythm",
            "Morning mein focus and one action read karo. Day mein relevant prompt apply karo. Evening mein five-minute reflection: kya hua, kya help hua, kya adjust karna hai. Notes short rakh sakte ho; pages par graphic details, illegal procurement information or someone else’s private data likhne ki zaroorat nahi. If keeping the book/device creates privacy risk, store securely or use clinician-approved alternative.",
            "Missed day = resume next day. Do not double exercises as punishment. If day’s action unrealistic, scale down: one contact instead of three, two-minute walk instead of workout, five lines instead of full page. Consistency with flexibility beats intensity with collapse. Mark ‘not applicable’ where needed; not every prompt fits every person.",
            exercise=("Daily minimum", ["Read today’s safety/focus line.", "Do one relevant action.", "Record one fact and one learning.", "Choose tomorrow’s one adjustment.", "Escalate if safety changed."]),
        ),
        S(
            "Weekly review: evidence, not mood only",
            "Days 7, 14, 21 and 30 par review karo. Mood fluctuates; evidence wider dekho. Kitne support contacts, appointments, pauses, safer choices, routine anchors, early warnings noticed? Any medical/relationship/financial risk? What improved in sleep, work, control, honesty? If nothing improved, that is also data — current plan/support may be inadequate, not proof you are hopeless.",
            "Review ke baad one keep, one change, one ask. Keep the useful action. Change one weak barrier. Ask for specific help. If repeated lapses or worsening symptoms, increase professional level of care rather than simply repeating workbook. Thirty days is a starting container, not biological reset or guaranteed cure.",
            bullets=["KEEP: which action earned continuation?", "CHANGE: which plan element did not fit real life?", "ASK: what professional/personal support is needed?", "ESCALATE: any safety issue requiring more than self-help?"],
        ),
        S(
            "Progress score without obsession",
            "Optional 0–2 rating: 0 = not done, 1 = attempted/partly done, 2 = completed. Rating effort and skill use ko capture karta hai, not moral worth. Do not total it into ‘good/bad person’ score. Weekly maximum/minimum chase mat karo. If numbers create anxiety or compulsive checking, stop scoring and use one sentence: ‘Aaj maine recovery ke liye ___ kiya.’",
            "Urges and behaviour counts can be clinically useful in some plans, but if they trigger shame or obsessive monitoring, clinician ke saath method choose karo. Functioning measures often more informative: attended work, slept safely, no risky situation, honest conversation, treatment adherence. Your workbook should serve your recovery; it must not become another ritual you have to perfectly perform.",
            callout=("Permission", "Aap blank space chhod sakte ho, prompt modify kar sakte ho, professional ke saath skip kar sakte ho. Safety and usefulness > completion."),
        ),
        S(
            "Day 30 ke baad",
            "Day 30 par decide karo: continue current rhythm, repeat a useful week, deepen therapy, or increase level of care. Do not automatically stop support because workbook finished. Build next 30 days with four weekly anchors and known high-risk dates. Schedule follow-up before motivation drops. Keep the lapse plan accessible.",
            "If the month was stable, acknowledge actions and people who helped. If it was difficult, acknowledge honesty and identify needed support. Both are legitimate starting points for next month. Long-term recovery is not a graduation certificate; it is continuing care and a life you do not need to escape repeatedly.",
            exercise=("Next-month commitment", ["My highest-value routine to continue.", "My biggest unresolved risk.", "Professional/support review date.", "One meaningful life goal beyond recovery.", "My early-warning and emergency plan location."]),
        ),
        refs=["R1", "R3", "R4", "R10", "R11", "R13", "R14", "R16", "R21", "R22", "R24"],
    ),
)
